<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Versailles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Versailles is a town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the site of the vast palace built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, which became the seat of the French court and a symbol of absolute monarchy. In 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1919 the peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was signed in the same hall, marking the end of the German Empire's era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/versailles.jpg" alt="Versailles" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ToucanWings · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_a%C3%A9rienne_du_domaine_de_Versailles_par_ToucanWings_-_Creative_Commons_By_Sa_3.0_-_081_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
